On 1/20/14, 7:00 PM, Michael Betancourt wrote:
>> is what I don't get.  How do these get stored in the stack?
>> We only went over functions that went from a small number of inputs
>> to a single output.  The matrix ops go from many inputs to many outputs.
>> If we had to store the Jacobian for the operation it'd be huge, even
>> at first order.  So I'm guessing you have some scheme to not store it.
>> But if so, how do you compute it in the sweeps?
>>
>> Maybe this is too low bandwidth a way to communicate --- we can wait
>> until the next meeting if we ever get one scheduled.
>

> No, you have to store it ï¿½ but you have to store it all no matter

> method you use (well, you have to compute it and allocate memory
> for it no matter which method you use, it just may be on the stack
> or on the heap).

I don't think so.  By doing multiple passes, you only wind up having
to compute a row of the Hessian at a time.  And in cases where the
expression graph is sparse, you don't store the equivalent of all the
zeroes --- just take dot product as an example again.

Of course, all the recomputation is where you lose out in time!

- Bob

