> I don't think so.  By doing multiple passes, you only wind up having
> to compute a row of the Hessian at a time.  And in cases where the
> expression graph is sparse, you don't store the equivalent of all the
> zeroes --- just take dot product as an example again.
>
> Of course, all the recomputation is where you lose out in time!

Again, we have competing issues here.

Virtual functions or static expression graphs would allow for
custom calculations at each node, providing a huge speed
up when there’s structure.  The latter is not mutually exclusive
with the new approach.

The alternative is to encode that structure into the
expression graph, as we have already discussed.

But general matrix ops do not have much structure —
there shouldn’t be any huge inefficiency using the new
approach verses the old one.

