I wouldn't get that shy about virtual functions.
You should do whatever's most efficient.

The thing I'm mainly worried about is memory in
the new approach.  Secondarily, I'm worried about
very sparse Hessians leading to too much extra work.

If you can tame that with virtual functions, it's
probably going to be worth taking the speed hit.

We could look into static expression graphs (Michael
means ones that don't change from evaluation to
evaluation, which means no loops or conditionals
based on anything other than data).

- Bob

