On 1/30/14, 6:08 PM, Marcus Brubaker wrote:
> I also think (3) is a bad idea.  I think those kinds of weird scoping rules just lead to unexpected surprises.  Plus, so
> far Stan has been pretty strict in it's language (e.g., requiring explicit declaration of variables) so (1) or (2)
> definitely seems more "natural" in that sense.
>
> Personally, i'd vote for (2) for a two reasons.  First, it's a familiar style of interface for ODE solvers so I think
> most people will be comfortable with it.  (1) is more expressive, but is going to be tricky to get right, e.g., what
> happens if a user defines constrained parameters, how are initial conditions passed in when there are multiple
> parameters, how is the result returned?  Second, I think in the process of implementing (2) it would be easy to at least
> lay the groundwork for more general user-defined functions in Stan which is a feature that's been on the wish-list for a
> long time and would help clean-up more complex models.

OK, I think everyone's in line with (2).

Of course, that system makes it seem easy.  When I was running it
by Andrew, I was using the system from a consulting project we're doing
that has around 10 parameters and marshaing/unmarshaling the vectors
is much more of a pain.

The plan is definitely to implement general functions first.

- Bob

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Thu Jan 30 17:53:05 2014
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Thu, 30 Jan 2014 17:53:05 +0000
Subject: function syntax and sizing
Message-ID: <wd1WmBAjC0c.9Ulrf4GwLYcJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/wd1WmBAjC0c/m/9Ulrf4GwLYcJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I figured the syntax for functions in Stan would be pretty
uncontentious.  They could all be defined before everything
else in a file if they don't depend on globals.

So function syntax would be something like:

   real foo(real x, real y) {
     return x * y + 2;
   }

Issues that remain in my mind:

1.  How to deal with local variables.

Is it OK to have them just at the top of blocks as we have now?
It was the lazy thing to do when I implemented blocks the first time, but I
sort of regret it because I like to declare variables near where
I use them.

2.  How to deal with array sizes.

Two possibilities seem obvious, the first of which is to
declare with no sizes, as in:

    real foo(vector x) {
      real sum;
      for (i in 1:size(x))
        sum <- sum + x[i];
      return sum;
    }

The other would be to require sizes, so we'd have:

   real foo(int<lower=0> N, vector[N] x) {
     real sum;
     for (i in 1:N)
       sum <- sum + x[i];
     return sum;
   }

And for the second option, it'd be easier to require sizes to appear
in the argument list before the vectors they size.

3.  How do we deal with multiple return values.

It's both awkward and inefficient that we now have two functions
for eigendecompisitions, eigenvectors and eigenvalues.  It'd be
nice to have a way to do something like Python does and return lists.

   (vector,matrix) eigendecompose(matrix x) {
      vector eigenval;
      matrix eigenvecs;
      eigenvals <- eigenvalues(x);
      eigenvecs <- eigenvectors(x);
      return (eigenvals,eigenvecs);
   }

I think that's going to be a pain to implement, though.  It's going
to require something like a general list type of heterogeneous types.
And most helpfuly a syntax like (x,y,z,...) to create a list.
The list would then implicitly have types, such as

    list[vector,matrix] eigendecomp;
    eigendecomp[1] <- eigenvals;
    eigendecomp[2] <- eigenvecs;
    return eigendecomp;

An alternative would be to have the function arguments be call-by-reference:

    void eigendecompose(matrix x, vector eigenvals, matrix eigenvecs) {
      eigenvals <- eigenvalues(x);
      eigenvecs <- eigenvectors(x);
    }

I was otherwise thinking I'd implement them by call-by-reference but with a const,
so they couldn't be modified.  But maybe there's no reason for the const?

- Bob

